WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.395
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.050
CHUCK RHOADES: So my
name is Chuck Rhoades.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:14.450
I am a senior manager of digital
infrastructure for Pizza Hut.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:15.950
And today, I'm
going to be talking

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:19.400
to you about microservices
born, raised,

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.130
and growing on Google Cloud.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:25.080
And so, for us, this is
really a customer journey.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.700
So we started working on
building out our service

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.930
infrastructure on Google
Cloud last summer--

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.650
so about a year ago.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.920
And we've been able to do a lot
in that time and move forward.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.420
So as it's a journey, we'll
kind of talk through it

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:42.840
in those phases.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.120
So to start with, we had a plan.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.110
We wanted to build
a microservice mesh

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:52.550
using containerized architecture
in a public cloud environment.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.610
And so, as we
started that, we knew

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.770
there were a few key
things we wanted to do.

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:01.190
So we wanted to use
Kubernetes for orchestration.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:02.960
And since this is a
Google conference,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.819
I'll just go right
ahead and assume

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:06.620
that we have all
agreed that Kubernetes

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:08.586
is the best orchestration tool.

00:01:08.586 --> 00:01:09.710
But we did do our research.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:11.180
We didn't just start there.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:12.650
We looked at other options.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.455
We looked at other technologies.

00:01:14.455 --> 00:01:15.830
And we really
found Kubernetes is

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:18.980
to be the best of
breed for serving

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:22.160
public workloads, private
workloads, and all the things

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.460
that we were going to need
to do in our architecture.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:28.730
Next, we were going to be
writing microservices in Go.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:31.100
Again, Google conference, so
I'll assume we all know Go

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.690
is the best
programming language.

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:34.440
But I'm not trying to
start a fight there,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.950
so its totally OK if
you disagree with me.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:40.520
But we really felt like
go has a lot of synergies

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.500
with container
architectures that

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:43.770
made it a really great fit.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.850
So the ability to compile
down to single buy-in areas

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.430
that you can deploy
into a container.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.480
It makes your
images really small.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.750
It makes your
deployments really small.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:55.790
And it lets you be
very efficient in how

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.650
you are deploying
those containers

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.120
into your architecture.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.580
Finally, we had settled on
deploying to Google Cloud.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.360
There were a lot of
reasons for that.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.234
There were a lot of
things that went into it.

00:02:08.234 --> 00:02:10.370
But key among those
was just simply

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:12.800
Google Kubernetes Engine,
which a year ago was

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:13.970
called the Container Engine.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:17.120
But it still had a K for
the name, so it works.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.990
It was really the best
way of deploying services

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:22.700
into a public
cloud environment--

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.800
not having to
manage masters, not

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.470
having to pay for your instances
that your masters are running

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:30.350
on, and really just the ability
to have all of the latest

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:34.104
features of Kubernetes available
in a very timely manner

00:02:34.104 --> 00:02:35.270
for deploying your services.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:40.100
So we had a really good idea of
what our core architecture was

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:41.850
going to look like.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.000
So before we kind
of move forward,

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.610
though, we really needed
to think about, what

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:49.640
actually is a microservice?

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.530
I've heard this term used
in a lot of different ways,

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.850
and I think the meaning
stays a little bit fuzzy.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:58.514
So as I was preparing
for this talk,

00:02:58.514 --> 00:02:59.930
I really started
to kind of second

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:01.730
guess my own ideas
about a microservice

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:03.590
and didn't really want
to be judged harshly.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.780
So I thought, let me go
use Google to find out

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:08.900
what the world says a
microservice is, and I couldn't

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.670
find a really great definition.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.340
I found kind of
paragraphs talking

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.620
around what
microservices are, but I

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.960
did find a set of
general concepts

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.180
that I think most
of us can agree on.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:22.370
Small independent services.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:25.250
Generally, we're deploying
those either into containers

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:29.000
or virtual machine architectures
with CI/CD pipelines.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.860
And ultimately,
those services are

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:33.710
interacting with one another
through service discovery.

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:35.820
And so, those are
good principles.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:39.110
Those are sort of good,
basic ways of describing

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:42.110
a microservice architecture,
but it still doesn't really

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:45.170
define what the actual
microservice is.

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:48.620
And I think there is
room there for us to have

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:50.310
different opinions about it.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:54.080
But for us, the really key
thing was service boundaries.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.080
Where are we drawing
the service boundaries?

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.080
Now, what we really
found to be great

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.930
guideposts were
sort of two rules.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.820
They're not hard and fast.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.400
We can have some
flexibility, but they sort of

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.260
set the upper and
lower boundaries

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.930
on how big we're going to make
an individual microservice.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:15.950
So we said every service
should be as small as possible

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:19.730
while maintaining meaningful
business function.

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:22.430
So when we're trying
to break services down,

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:24.950
I think we've probably all
at some point in our career--

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:26.991
for the software
developers among us--

00:04:26.991 --> 00:04:29.240
we've made that mistake of
making a service too small,

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:30.830
and we eventually
found we've just

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:34.070
created an API that we have
to write SQL in or something

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:35.100
like that.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.880
So there is a lower boundary
that is meaningful business

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:38.690
function.

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:40.310
But on the other
side, we want to make

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:42.470
sure that every one
of those services

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.240
fully and completely
encapsulates

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.690
the data and the function
that it's responsible for.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:50.540
It can be easy to have some
of these things sort of creep

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.480
in to our service
architectures where

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.520
we allow our data
to be interacted

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.862
with by some other kind of batch
process or something like that.

00:04:58.862 --> 00:05:00.570
And almost always, it
gets us in trouble.

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:02.540
So we define these as
sort of our guideposts.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:06.300
This is what we wanted to stay
between for our microservice

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:07.534
architecture.

00:05:07.534 --> 00:05:09.450
So the last thing we
really had to think about

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:12.540
before we could get started
was, what kind of services

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.640
are we going to build?

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.200
REST is an obvious choice.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.661
It's absolutely ubiquitous.

00:05:18.661 --> 00:05:20.910
When we looked at the people
who are most likely going

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.370
to be consuming the
services we're building,

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.920
this is what they would
be expecting to consume.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.360
They would be expecting JSON web
services over REST protocols.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:33.660
So it was a very
good, obvious target.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:35.170
Also, REST is well supported.

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:37.950
I mean, we all know every
programming language

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:39.660
you probably want
to use, you're going

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:43.260
to be able to interact with a
REST service pretty readily.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.930
The downside with
REST, though, for us

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:51.150
was that there's really no
standard core message contract

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.110
when it comes to REST services.

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:55.710
And fortunately,
recently, OpenAPI

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:57.810
has really sort of
become a de facto way

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:00.370
of defining those services
and interacting with them,

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.630
but it's still an
after-the-fact specification,

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:07.170
and we're relying on the
effort of the developer

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:10.590
and sort of our reliability to
be rigorous in how we define

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:13.530
our services to have those
OpenAPI specs actually

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.090
mean anything.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.010
We've probably
had the experience

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:19.320
of interacting with
an API that said

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.710
it was going to do one thing
and then it did something else,

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:25.530
and it causes all sorts of bugs
downstream when you're trying

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.130
to consume those services.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:29.780
So the other option
we looked at was gRPC,

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:31.950
and we found a
lot to love there.

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:33.930
As you can probably
guess at this point,

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:36.150
message contract is
really important to us.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.290
It was something we
were really focused on.

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:42.130
And so having protobufs as
the definition of an RPC

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:46.230
be kind of the core piece of
how gRPCs are put together

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:47.790
was a really attractive thing.

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:50.160
And so, if you haven't
had experience with gRPC,

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.110
you define those with
a protobuf document.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.450
It's a very clear,
concise definition.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:57.510
And then, you use a tool called
protoc to generate your stubs

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.607
for servers and clients,
documentation, lots of things

00:07:00.607 --> 00:07:01.440
through the plugins.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.980
And so, through that, we also
see great tooling around gRPC.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:07.020
So it was definitely
something we

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:09.450
wanted to target in
our architecture,

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:12.460
but the problem we had was
it's just not as common yet.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:14.220
It's still new enough.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:16.830
It's still not something
that every consumer who's

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:19.750
going to pull in your service is
going to be ready to deal with.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:23.010
And so we wanted to make
sure that our services were

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:24.594
friendly to our
consumers, but we also

00:07:24.594 --> 00:07:26.760
wanted to be able to make
the best technical choices

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:28.470
in our architecture.

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:30.570
And so that sort of
leads to how we came up

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:34.620
with a very simple overview
of how we were going

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.840
to approach our service mesh.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:40.860
We drew a big distinction
between a publicly-exposed API

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.190
and internal services and in
the microservice architecture.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:46.020
So we said, for all
of our services,

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:47.610
for all of our business logic--

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.830
really the meaningful
bits of what we're doing--

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:53.120
we're going to keep
those as gRPC services,

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:55.590
and we're going to put those
behind cluster IP services

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:56.970
and Kubernetes.

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:59.867
So if you're familiar with
that, cluster IP services

00:07:59.867 --> 00:08:01.450
are only available
within the cluster,

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:03.060
which means it's not exposed.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:05.400
There's no public
IT accessibility.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.800
But all of those services
can talk to one another.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:10.136
Service discovery works--
all of those things.

00:08:10.136 --> 00:08:12.510
And then, we're going to build
a separate kind of service

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:15.120
in our cluster for
our REST servers

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:18.750
that is based off of an open
API specification that is a REST

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:22.050
API that we can expose
via a load balancer server

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.520
out to the rest of the world.

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:27.210
The other nice benefit we
get in that arrangement

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:30.360
is each of those
individual gRPC servers

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.669
can fully encapsulate the
data they're responsible for.

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:35.280
So we can follow our
service boundary rules,

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.710
meaning they have the
administrative points

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:39.299
and functionality in them.

00:08:39.299 --> 00:08:41.429
But whenever we create
those REST servers

00:08:41.429 --> 00:08:43.500
and we define that
in open API, we

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:45.810
can only pull up the
methods that we actually

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:48.400
want to expose out to
a broader audience.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:49.890
So this was our idea.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:51.890
This is where we wanted to go.

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:55.200
And we felt like we had
a really solid plan.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.380
So the next step on our
journey, we had a project--

00:08:58.380 --> 00:08:59.700
location, location, location.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:03.330
So hopefully everyone in this
room shops with Pizza Hut

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:06.614
all the time and you buy
our products online a lot.

00:09:06.614 --> 00:09:09.030
And so, as I talk through this,
it will sort of make sense

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:12.240
and you'll kind of recognize
it in your experience.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:16.770
But our customers need to be
able to find stores online

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:18.720
whenever they want to
place an order with us.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:22.300
And at this point in time, when
this project came to my team,

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:25.260
we were at a place where
Pizza Hut was in a transition.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:28.620
We were moving away from a
previous mapping provider

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:32.180
over to Google Maps for all
of our geocoding and customer

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:34.350
address lookup and
things like that.

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.150
It provided an
opportunity for us

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:39.720
to also retire an
older service that we

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:42.930
were using to help with
some of that store location

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.430
and delivery lookup
stuff and replace that.

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:47.250
So that was our project.

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.860
When we bring projects
into our team,

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.979
we really like to look at it
from a few specific questions,

00:09:52.979 --> 00:09:54.270
and we start with the customer.

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:56.790
What do our customers
need to be able to do?

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:59.490
And for this service, they need
to be able to do two things--

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:02.700
find all the stores in range
of a particular location

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.830
and find a single store
that will deliver pizza

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:06.380
to their location.

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:08.380
So these are the two kind
of methods that we see

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:10.480
evolving out of this project.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:12.820
And then, we look at the
aspects of the service.

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:14.715
What attributes are
really important?

00:10:14.715 --> 00:10:16.090
And this is where
we kind of look

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:19.250
at usage patterns and traffic
patterns on the service.

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:22.280
So for this one, we need to
return answers really fast.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:25.840
We have a very high read load
because those customers are

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:27.280
searching for their location.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:28.510
That's being looked up.

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:30.050
We also need high availability.

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:32.601
This is the first step to a
customer interacting with us

00:10:32.601 --> 00:10:33.100
online.

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:35.410
We want to make sure that's
really available and really

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:37.660
present and we don't
interrupt that ability

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.610
to place orders with us.

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:42.960
And finally, we have a low
read load for the service.

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:45.400
We only need to update
data about once an hour.

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:48.224
Our service is not the system
of record for the things

00:10:48.224 --> 00:10:48.890
that it's using.

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:51.340
It's really serving as
an online accessibility

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:52.990
point and more of a cache.

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:56.519
So we took that service
architecture we started with

00:10:56.519 --> 00:10:58.060
and we said, OK,
how do we break down

00:10:58.060 --> 00:10:59.860
the service we need to build?

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:01.660
The first thing was
our service boundaries.

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:04.510
So we started looking at what
RPCs are going to implement,

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:08.890
and we decided we would have
one that's focused on store

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:11.470
information-- so all of the
things you need to know when

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:13.570
you find your store, as far as
where it is or what's its phone

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:14.300
number--

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:18.080
and a separate one that would be
focused on location type data--

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:20.350
so this would be the thing
that's doing our geoqueries

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:21.719
and handling that information.

00:11:21.719 --> 00:11:23.510
And when you look at
it, you may say, well,

00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:25.010
it's all really
still about storage,

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:27.410
so why isn't this one service?

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:28.840
And we had that
debate internally.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:31.299
And actually, in an early
prototype, it was one service.

00:11:31.299 --> 00:11:33.340
But again, we look back
at our service boundaries

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:34.756
and how we wanted
to define those.

00:11:34.756 --> 00:11:37.809
And we said, we want to make
the services as small as we can

00:11:37.809 --> 00:11:39.350
while maintaining
business functions.

00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:41.710
So we can break these
in two, so we're

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:43.610
going to go ahead and do that.

00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:46.570
So we had to backend
RPC services supporting

00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:49.210
all the methods that we need
to interact with our data,

00:11:49.210 --> 00:11:51.340
and then we pulled up
those two functions--

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:53.590
finding your delivery
store and finding

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:57.550
stores near a particular
location-- into our REST API.

00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:00.440
But no service works
without data behind it,

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:04.240
so we needed to solve
for data storage.

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:06.700
And I've been around for a
little while, long enough

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:09.880
to go from talking about gray
beards to having a lot of gray

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:10.450
in my beard.

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:13.930
So I definitely come from
that architectural approach

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:16.529
where we would start
with just one database,

00:12:16.529 --> 00:12:18.070
and we kind of throw
everything at it

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:20.680
and kind of torque
every use case

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:23.510
we had to fit an RDBMS
system and things like that.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:25.060
So we didn't want
to do that here.

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:26.677
We're in the cloud now.

00:12:26.677 --> 00:12:28.760
We should be able to do
things in a different way.

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:31.526
So we wanted to pick our
data storage solution based

00:12:31.526 --> 00:12:32.650
on the requirements we had.

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:36.371
So we said we need
high speed reads.

00:12:36.371 --> 00:12:38.620
We have queries that are
going to be very key-centric.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:41.740
Our location service is
taking care of indexing

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:43.516
and a lot of the
geospatial stuff

00:12:43.516 --> 00:12:45.640
and really gets us down to
a point where we're just

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:49.000
doing very key-centric
reads, so we

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.040
need something that can
handle that really well.

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:52.870
And then, we need
multi-region scale--

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:55.115
again, high availability
for our customers.

00:12:55.115 --> 00:12:56.740
We want to make sure
that whatever data

00:12:56.740 --> 00:13:00.250
solution we're using has that
same availability kind of thing

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:01.240
to it.

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:04.106
So as we looked at the
options that were out there,

00:13:04.106 --> 00:13:05.980
we also looked at some
of the other offerings

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:06.740
in Google Cloud.

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:09.750
We're only using Kubernetes
Engine at this point.

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:13.780
And Datastore actually
solved really a lot

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:16.300
of the main concerns we had.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:18.009
It will scale to
hundreds of thousands

00:13:18.009 --> 00:13:20.050
of read operations per
second, so that definitely

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:22.810
will cover the scale we need.

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:25.850
The key query performance
is amazingly fast,

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:28.190
and so that was going
to be a really good fit.

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:29.710
And it's just
multi-region built-in.

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:32.050
Whenever you provision
out your data store,

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:34.450
you choose a
multi-region region,

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:37.390
and you're up and running and
you have multi-region support.

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:40.764
So we added Datastore to
the backend of our cluster.

00:13:40.764 --> 00:13:42.430
We're kind of starting
to pick up steam.

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:43.429
We have a full solution.

00:13:43.429 --> 00:13:44.950
We actually can
send queries to it.

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:46.900
We can do things with it.

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:49.000
But we need to
think about, how are

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:50.590
we going to get our data in?

00:13:50.590 --> 00:13:53.020
And so this is really,
like I said, a journey.

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:54.670
We didn't know
everything going in.

00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:56.626
When we looked at
Kubernetes Engine,

00:13:56.626 --> 00:13:58.000
we were really
only looking at it

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:01.060
from sort of that
very specific lane of,

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:04.032
I'm going to have queries
coming in from the web.

00:14:04.032 --> 00:14:05.740
I need to service
those and return those.

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:08.270
We weren't thinking about
other kinds of workloads.

00:14:08.270 --> 00:14:11.170
So we knew we could put our
source data into a Cloud

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:13.840
Storage bucket, but
how are we going to get

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:15.430
it loaded into our databases?

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:19.240
And that's where, fortunately
as we looked through Kubernetes,

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:22.300
we found there's a workload
specifically for that.

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:25.160
Kubernetes has the
concept of a job,

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:27.430
and so we could simply
create that job.

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:29.082
We could schedule it to run.

00:14:29.082 --> 00:14:30.790
And one of the great
side effects of that

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:33.340
was, being that the job
is running in cluster,

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:35.530
we don't have to worry
about extending the methods

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:38.230
that job needs to operate
outside of the cluster

00:14:38.230 --> 00:14:39.040
boundary.

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:42.070
So that worked out
for us really well.

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:44.690
But then, we needed to
think about API management.

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:46.870
So we have a service
that ultimately we

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:48.810
are going to be
exposing publicly,

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:50.945
and we are going to
have consumers outside

00:14:50.945 --> 00:14:51.820
of Pizza Hut hitting.

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:56.260
So we need the proper security
and management around that.

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:57.760
For this particular
service, we were

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:01.360
going to use API keys as the
appropriate way of securing

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:04.930
the API, but we also wanted
support for JWT tokens.

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:06.789
We knew that we would
be building services

00:15:06.789 --> 00:15:09.205
that we would have kind of a
higher need for authorisation

00:15:09.205 --> 00:15:10.270
on.

00:15:10.270 --> 00:15:13.391
We also wanted usage reports
and performance statistics--

00:15:13.391 --> 00:15:15.640
what's happening with the
service as we're using them.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:18.140
And we want to integrate
with those OpenAPI specs.

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:21.619
We made such a big deal about
having that message contract,

00:15:21.619 --> 00:15:24.160
we wanted to make sure whatever
API management platform we're

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:26.140
using integrates with that.

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:28.740
Again, we looked at the service
offerings in Google Cloud.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:30.490
We compared that
to other options.

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:32.620
And we really settled
on Cloud Endpoints

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:34.900
as being a really
great fit for where

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:39.370
we were in building and
working on our API platform.

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:42.430
So Cloud Endpoints has a
really, really flexible set

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:43.960
of security options.

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:46.330
It's configured declaratively.

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:48.040
Obviously, it supports API keys.

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:49.567
It supports JWT tokens.

00:15:49.567 --> 00:15:51.400
And it has pluggable
backend, so you can use

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:52.816
[? Google's ?]
[? off ?] for that.

00:15:52.816 --> 00:15:54.180
You can use your own.

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:56.440
There's a rich dashboard
integrated directly

00:15:56.440 --> 00:15:58.870
into your Cloud console
for your Cloud Endpoints,

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:02.507
so you can very easily
look at usage by API keys.

00:16:02.507 --> 00:16:04.090
You can see the
performance statistics

00:16:04.090 --> 00:16:06.340
across your API endpoints
and a lot of the things

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:07.600
we needed to see.

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:09.820
And finally, it is
configured and deployed

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:11.000
with OpenAPI specs.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:13.450
So just by tweaking
a couple of things

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:16.120
in a couple of places on
our API specifications,

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:20.115
we were able to just immediately
deploy the Cloud Endpoint.

00:16:20.115 --> 00:16:21.490
I do want to zoom
in a little bit

00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:23.864
on one of the workloads we've
been talking about, though.

00:16:23.864 --> 00:16:25.960
So that REST service
that we have deployed,

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:29.200
when we look at the kind of
Kubernetes components of that,

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:30.770
we have a Kubernetes
service that's

00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:33.850
the load balancer
targeting port 443.

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:37.075
And then, we have a REST
API, a Kubernetes deployment.

00:16:37.075 --> 00:16:38.950
And at the time, we just
had a single service

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:41.866
in the pod, our REST
server that we built.

00:16:41.866 --> 00:16:43.240
The great thing
with implementing

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:46.180
Cloud Endpoints was it was
really just a pretty minor

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:48.010
change to that workload.

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:52.570
You add a declaration for the
ESP container to your pod.

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:54.694
And the ESP is the
Extensible Service Proxy.

00:16:54.694 --> 00:16:56.110
That's actually
the component that

00:16:56.110 --> 00:16:57.417
implements Cloud Endpoints.

00:16:57.417 --> 00:16:59.375
And then, we just made
a little port adjustment

00:16:59.375 --> 00:17:02.140
so our REST server is on
a separate port that's

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:05.020
only available on the pod.

00:17:05.020 --> 00:17:08.020
And then, the service
container can proxy to it.

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:11.560
We're only exposing the service
through a protected channel,

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:14.211
and we didn't really have to
change our architecture that

00:17:14.211 --> 00:17:14.710
much.

00:17:14.710 --> 00:17:17.140
We just added endpoints
to our REST containers,

00:17:17.140 --> 00:17:19.790
and we were in good
shape and ready to go.

00:17:19.790 --> 00:17:23.079
So next step, we needed to
think about high availability.

00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:26.770
So we've kind of talked about
how we crafted the cluster

00:17:26.770 --> 00:17:29.560
and how we put that together,
but we're still really only

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:32.110
talking about a single cluster.

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:34.720
We needed better
availability than that.

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:37.910
So we said, first of all, we can
use a multi-availability zone

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:38.410
cluster.

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:41.650
That's a feature of Google
Kubernetes Engine where

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:45.547
you can extend your cluster into
separate availability zones,

00:17:45.547 --> 00:17:47.380
and it will distribute
your pods accordingly

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:50.289
so that you have availability
across all of those zones.

00:17:50.289 --> 00:17:52.330
At the time we were doing
this, regional clusters

00:17:52.330 --> 00:17:54.520
were not a thing
yet, which are now,

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:56.020
and that's probably
what we would do

00:17:56.020 --> 00:17:58.450
and what we actually
will do going forward.

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:01.030
But even then, we're
still in a single region,

00:18:01.030 --> 00:18:02.590
and you're still
leaving yourself

00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:04.120
a certain amount
of exposure anytime

00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:06.820
you're building architecture
in a single cloud region.

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:10.330
So what we really wanted was
to have multi-AZ clusters

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:11.440
in multi-region.

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:12.520
So we have east.

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:13.630
We have west.

00:18:13.630 --> 00:18:15.610
We have across
availability zones.

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:17.980
But when we do
that, we need a way

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:22.434
to get traffic into those
zones, into those regions.

00:18:22.434 --> 00:18:24.100
Unfortunately, we
don't have the ability

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:26.500
to do cross-regional
clustering quite yet,

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:30.002
so we needed something that
would distribute that traffic.

00:18:30.002 --> 00:18:31.960
And if you've been using
Google Cloud for long,

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:34.459
you'll probably predict kind
of where we're going with this.

00:18:34.459 --> 00:18:36.730
But we needed to be able
to distribute traffic

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:37.967
into our cluster.

00:18:37.967 --> 00:18:40.300
We also wanted to make sure
that we're serving customers

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:42.430
from the closest
cluster to them.

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:44.980
So for geographic
availability, you

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:48.042
can be served in San
Fran out of a data center

00:18:48.042 --> 00:18:50.500
on the East Coast, but we would
rather that not be the case

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:51.870
if it doesn't have to be.

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:54.730
And finally, we need to
remove unhealthy clusters

00:18:54.730 --> 00:18:56.410
from the services automatically.

00:18:56.410 --> 00:18:59.830
So Google Load Balancer,
the Global Load Balancer,

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:00.930
solved this.

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:03.460
And it really was just a direct
fit for the use case we had.

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:07.740
It's designed for
multi-region load balancing.

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:10.150
It distributes load
geographically to the nearest

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:14.230
backend by default. And it
has support for health checks,

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:17.680
so you can make sure that your
services come in and go out--

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:21.070
or your backends for your
services as the health profile

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:22.600
changes.

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:25.645
So we put the Google Cloud
Global Load Balancer--

00:19:25.645 --> 00:19:27.520
I always say the Google
Cloud Load Balancer--

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:29.230
but we put the
Global Load Balancer

00:19:29.230 --> 00:19:31.250
in front of our customers.

00:19:31.250 --> 00:19:33.100
And ultimately, our
architecture starts

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:34.400
to look a little like this.

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:36.010
Now, we did have
to make one change.

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:38.710
Today, the Global Load
Balancer does not really

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:42.190
recognize the Kubernetes
cluster as a thing.

00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:46.510
It recognizes it as
instance pools of compute.

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:49.180
So the way around that
is to create a node port

00:19:49.180 --> 00:19:52.420
service in Kubernetes
that the Cloud Load

00:19:52.420 --> 00:19:54.317
Balancer can address.

00:19:54.317 --> 00:19:56.650
Fortunately, that's really
pretty straightforward to do.

00:19:56.650 --> 00:19:58.480
You create your
node port service.

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:00.310
We left our load
balancer service in place

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:03.040
because we found it was still
beneficial to be able to route

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:06.630
traffic directly to regions
for testing and seeing

00:20:06.630 --> 00:20:08.310
what's going on there.

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:09.840
But it got us to
this point where

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:13.680
we had sort of fully
rationalized and fleshed

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:16.470
out architecture, so
we implemented it,

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:17.730
and we turned it on.

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:19.350
And it worked really well.

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:21.810
It actually served
traffic really well.

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:25.200
This was in place last year
through the Super Bowl,

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:26.555
which is our Super Bowl.

00:20:26.555 --> 00:20:28.680
A lot of businesses talk
about what day of the year

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:29.730
is their Super Bowl.

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:31.680
For Pizza Hut, it is
actually the Super Bowl.

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:34.140
So this did a really great job.

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:35.790
It performed really well.

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:39.240
The architecture scaled as we
hoped and expected it would.

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:42.000
But along the way,
what did we learn?

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:43.950
And the answer would be a lot.

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:45.240
We learned a lot of things.

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:47.520
And I think most
of what I've been

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:49.080
talking about up to
this point really

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:50.880
was us learning as we went--

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:53.269
learning that we needed
API management, what

00:20:53.269 --> 00:20:54.810
was that going to
look like, learning

00:20:54.810 --> 00:20:56.776
about needing load
balancing, what

00:20:56.776 --> 00:20:57.900
is that going to look like.

00:20:57.900 --> 00:21:01.650
But I really want to highlight
a few specific things

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:03.990
sort of from we were right--

00:21:03.990 --> 00:21:06.186
we learned-- to we were wrong.

00:21:06.186 --> 00:21:08.560
And so we'll start with we
were right because it's always

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:10.050
great to start on a high note.

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:12.690
gRPCs with well-defined
service boundaries

00:21:12.690 --> 00:21:14.430
worked really, really well.

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:18.870
So as of right now, we are
going through a lot of upgrades

00:21:18.870 --> 00:21:22.740
and some feature changes to
some of those early services we

00:21:22.740 --> 00:21:25.650
built. And what we've
really been happy to find

00:21:25.650 --> 00:21:27.900
is that, by having
those service boundaries

00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:29.970
and having them
written in RPCs, we

00:21:29.970 --> 00:21:32.460
can really trust
those protobufs to be

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:34.389
the thing we need to target.

00:21:34.389 --> 00:21:36.430
The experience a lot of
us as software developers

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:38.610
have had of just
implement the interface

00:21:38.610 --> 00:21:40.630
and your implementation
will be fine--

00:21:40.630 --> 00:21:43.770
mostly-- we do get the
ability to carry that

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:45.030
into our service architecture.

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:47.072
So we can say,
implement the protobuf,

00:21:47.072 --> 00:21:48.780
and then do all your
performance and load

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:50.190
testing everything you need.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:51.870
But you can trust
that your consumers

00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:54.720
are going to be OK because
you implemented the protobuf.

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:57.660
So that worked
really, really well.

00:21:57.660 --> 00:21:59.650
What we were kind of pleased--

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:02.310
I don't know if surprised is the
right word, maybe delighted--

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:04.200
but Stackdriver
actually turned out

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:06.690
to be a really amazing
toolset for us.

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:09.270
We knew, in a
distributed architecture,

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:10.920
we're going to need
to be able to know

00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:13.320
what's going on
with our services

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:14.920
and how's everything performing.

00:22:14.920 --> 00:22:20.010
So having Stackdriver
logging and tracing available

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:22.890
was really a huge benefit to us.

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:26.040
From the very beginning, we
were structuring our logs

00:22:26.040 --> 00:22:28.890
so that, when they did hit
whatever downstream log system

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:32.630
they would go to, they would
be searchable and accessible.

00:22:32.630 --> 00:22:34.380
The integration between
Kubernetes Engines

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:36.754
and Stackdriver, though, just
sort of made all that work.

00:22:36.754 --> 00:22:38.430
We just write the log
out like we always

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:41.050
would with whatever
components we want,

00:22:41.050 --> 00:22:44.040
and they show up in
Stackdriver fully broken

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:46.980
down so that we can search,
we can create metrics,

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:48.560
and do all of those things.

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:50.787
And then, tracing,
we did see that we

00:22:50.787 --> 00:22:52.870
needed to do distributed
trace from the beginning.

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:54.426
So we said, we'll
implement this.

00:22:54.426 --> 00:22:55.800
We'll put it in
all our services.

00:22:55.800 --> 00:23:00.750
And we'll wrap all of our
callouts in a trace boundary.

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:03.550
And we'll be able to kind of see
how the service is performing.

00:23:03.550 --> 00:23:06.240
And so it was kind of
cool to look at the charts

00:23:06.240 --> 00:23:09.180
and pull it to the beginning,
and so it was kind of fine.

00:23:09.180 --> 00:23:12.960
But then, we noticed on one
of the services in particular

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:16.020
that about a third
of the traffic

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:18.750
was significantly more
latent than the rest

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:20.880
of the traffic in the service--

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:23.050
and so much so that
was really noticeable.

00:23:23.050 --> 00:23:24.767
When we looked at the
performance graph,

00:23:24.767 --> 00:23:26.100
you sort of had these two peaks.

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:28.480
There was one big glut
of all the traffic

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:31.620
and then like 100-millisecond
delay and a second peak.

00:23:31.620 --> 00:23:32.980
And so that was problematic.

00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:36.060
Why is a third of our
traffic so much slower

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:37.050
than the other traffic?

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:39.820
And the good news was it
was really easy to find out.

00:23:39.820 --> 00:23:43.470
We just simply went in, and we
clicked on a few of the traces.

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:46.680
And fortunately, we had wrapped
the problem area in a boundary.

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:48.750
And so we saw there
was a callout.

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:51.150
There was a place in
that service where,

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:53.070
under certain
conditions, we would

00:23:53.070 --> 00:23:56.190
call to geocode a particular
piece of information.

00:23:56.190 --> 00:23:57.900
And it was something
that was cacheable.

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:00.060
It wasn't really
customer specific.

00:24:00.060 --> 00:24:03.066
But it's one of those
situations where,

00:24:03.066 --> 00:24:04.440
because it was
sort of downstream

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:06.424
of so many other things,
it just kind of got

00:24:06.424 --> 00:24:08.340
wrapped in a library and
wrapped in a library,

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:11.550
and we didn't really count
the cost of the call anymore.

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:14.310
But through tracing, we
were able to identify it.

00:24:14.310 --> 00:24:16.560
We switched that over
so that we did properly

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:18.420
cache that response.

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:20.449
And we got rid of
that second peak

00:24:20.449 --> 00:24:22.240
and moved all of our
traffic back together.

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:26.920
So from there, we loved
tracing even more.

00:24:26.920 --> 00:24:29.010
Then, we noticed
actually it's better

00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:32.400
than we thought because tracing
is great across our services,

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:34.320
but it also integrates
to Cloud Endpoints

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:35.970
and it integrates to Datastore.

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:39.600
So now, I have a very rich
amount of detail all the way

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:43.260
from the point where traffic
enters into my endpoint, what

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:46.140
Cloud Endpoints does with it,
what latency that introduces,

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:47.640
all the way through
my architecture,

00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:49.960
and then what's happening
in the database--

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:53.010
which is a really wonderful
amount of visibility

00:24:53.010 --> 00:24:56.170
into what's going on
with our services.

00:24:56.170 --> 00:24:59.040
And finally, we also learned
that our REST layer that we

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:01.220
talked about, it does work.

00:25:01.220 --> 00:25:02.820
It lets us do the
things we wanted

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:07.010
to do as far as pulling
services up out of the RPCs

00:25:07.010 --> 00:25:09.020
for exposing those
to public, but it

00:25:09.020 --> 00:25:11.450
gets a bit boilerplate-ish.

00:25:11.450 --> 00:25:13.100
Most of the time as
we're implementing

00:25:13.100 --> 00:25:15.470
that, we're sort
of just recreating

00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:18.110
a call, an OpenAPI that we
already created in protobuf,

00:25:18.110 --> 00:25:20.120
and passing it through.

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:23.460
And developers love
writing boilerplate,

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:25.412
so we left it in
place for a while.

00:25:25.412 --> 00:25:26.870
But then, they told
me I was wrong.

00:25:26.870 --> 00:25:28.370
They don't love
writing boilerplate,

00:25:28.370 --> 00:25:30.440
so we started looking
at other solutions.

00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:35.840
And that kind of takes us into
where are we going from here.

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:38.720
So where are we going?

00:25:38.720 --> 00:25:41.480
We have several different areas
that we're looking into now.

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:44.600
So one of those is Istio.

00:25:44.600 --> 00:25:47.330
And if you're looking for
me during the conference,

00:25:47.330 --> 00:25:48.560
go to any of the Istio talks.

00:25:48.560 --> 00:25:50.268
You'll probably find
me there because I'm

00:25:50.268 --> 00:25:52.130
attending as many as I can.

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:54.950
We really think
Istio is going to be

00:25:54.950 --> 00:25:58.050
an amazing expansion to what we
do in container architectures.

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:00.050
So if you've worked
in Kubernetes,

00:26:00.050 --> 00:26:04.052
you know it does do a really
great job of managing services,

00:26:04.052 --> 00:26:06.260
but there are still some
things you're doing yourself

00:26:06.260 --> 00:26:10.250
or some things that are left
to the developer to implement.

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:14.110
So Istio solves a lot
of those challenges.

00:26:14.110 --> 00:26:16.280
So one of the things just
with proxying traffic

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:18.260
throughout your cluster,
if you implement

00:26:18.260 --> 00:26:20.870
an architecture like we
have, you'll see gRPC

00:26:20.870 --> 00:26:22.640
is an HTTP/2 service.

00:26:22.640 --> 00:26:25.100
And so some of you may have
been thinking as it is going,

00:26:25.100 --> 00:26:26.600
like, how did you
solve that problem

00:26:26.600 --> 00:26:30.020
of proxying gRPC traffic
inside of Kubernetes?

00:26:30.020 --> 00:26:33.220
And with a lot of work,
that's how we solved it.

00:26:33.220 --> 00:26:34.550
Istio has that built into it.

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:37.340
It is HTTP/2 aware
whenever it's distributing

00:26:37.340 --> 00:26:39.710
traffic inside of the
cluster, and so that's

00:26:39.710 --> 00:26:41.209
a really huge benefit.

00:26:41.209 --> 00:26:43.250
The other thing is, once
you have an architecture

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:46.550
sort of scaled out the way ours
is, you start to have routing

00:26:46.550 --> 00:26:47.880
at a lot of different places.

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:49.700
You have some DNS
stuff going on.

00:26:49.700 --> 00:26:52.460
You have some routing in
your Global Load Balancer.

00:26:52.460 --> 00:26:54.082
Each of your Kubernetes
load balancers

00:26:54.082 --> 00:26:55.790
are implementing their
own load balancer,

00:26:55.790 --> 00:26:57.800
and so you have routing in that.

00:26:57.800 --> 00:27:00.544
And you ultimately kind of have
how your services are routing

00:27:00.544 --> 00:27:02.960
to and through the clusters
is a little bit kind of spread

00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:03.620
around.

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:07.325
With Istio, you can declare
all of that routing in-cluster

00:27:07.325 --> 00:27:09.450
and have it really take
care of managing everything

00:27:09.450 --> 00:27:11.780
once it makes it into
the mesh ingress.

00:27:11.780 --> 00:27:14.540
And finally, mutual TLS
for service-to-service

00:27:14.540 --> 00:27:15.230
authentication.

00:27:15.230 --> 00:27:17.000
So we've really
been looking a lot

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:18.500
at how we're doing
defense in-depth

00:27:18.500 --> 00:27:20.110
and where we want
to go with that,

00:27:20.110 --> 00:27:22.400
and Istio just meshes
in really well with what

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:24.260
we want to achieve there.

00:27:24.260 --> 00:27:26.450
Next, so we talked
about that problem

00:27:26.450 --> 00:27:29.090
of having those
sort of REST APIs.

00:27:29.090 --> 00:27:32.060
And the main use case
we had for that was we

00:27:32.060 --> 00:27:33.470
need to be able
to kind of cherry

00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:36.380
pick specific
methods out of RPCs,

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:39.320
mix those together,
and sometimes transform

00:27:39.320 --> 00:27:41.360
the requests and
responses away from what

00:27:41.360 --> 00:27:43.580
we would do in the
RPC to something

00:27:43.580 --> 00:27:47.300
that's more specific to
a particular consumer.

00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:49.070
And so, really,
what I've described

00:27:49.070 --> 00:27:52.440
there is a lot of what
Apigee actually does.

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:53.870
So as we have
matured our services

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:55.911
and we've looked at moving
into a little bit more

00:27:55.911 --> 00:27:59.589
of a full-featured
API management suite,

00:27:59.589 --> 00:28:00.880
we're moving in that direction.

00:28:00.880 --> 00:28:04.240
And so, again, as I was
talking about gRPC and REST,

00:28:04.240 --> 00:28:06.556
some of you guys probably
thought about, why didn't you

00:28:06.556 --> 00:28:08.180
look at the gRPC
gateway because that's

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:09.350
pretty much what it does?

00:28:09.350 --> 00:28:10.730
It takes your protobuf.

00:28:10.730 --> 00:28:13.320
It compiles out a
REST proxy for that.

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:14.844
And so that's
actually the direction

00:28:14.844 --> 00:28:16.010
we're going to be going now.

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:20.510
So we'll be wrapping everything
in gRPC gateway proxies.

00:28:20.510 --> 00:28:24.070
That will expose those as
APIs up into our Apigee layer.

00:28:24.070 --> 00:28:26.471
And then, from there,
we will be able to pick

00:28:26.471 --> 00:28:27.470
which endpoints we want.

00:28:27.470 --> 00:28:30.320
We'll combine those into
products within Apigee

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:32.840
and do the transformations
and all of the things

00:28:32.840 --> 00:28:34.950
we need to do there.

00:28:34.950 --> 00:28:37.010
And then, where
else are we going?

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:39.455
More and more and more
and more services.

00:28:39.455 --> 00:28:41.330
So I really kind of
talked through a use case

00:28:41.330 --> 00:28:42.710
with two services.

00:28:42.710 --> 00:28:44.690
We've grown well past that.

00:28:44.690 --> 00:28:47.300
We've had several other
projects where we've introduced

00:28:47.300 --> 00:28:48.355
many additional services.

00:28:48.355 --> 00:28:50.480
And we look a little bit
more like that initial map

00:28:50.480 --> 00:28:53.670
where not everything is just a
service exposed through a REST.

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:55.970
We have things that are purely
internal to the cluster.

00:28:55.970 --> 00:28:59.840
We have things that are wrapping
calls out to the broader world.

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:01.790
We actually brought in
other cloud products.

00:29:01.790 --> 00:29:04.370
So I didn't have a chance
in this particular use case

00:29:04.370 --> 00:29:06.410
to talk about Cloud
Pub/Sub, but that's

00:29:06.410 --> 00:29:09.520
become a really significant part
of our architecture as well,

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:11.040
and we found
anywhere that we need

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:14.240
to do an asynchronous
payload it's fit extremely

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:16.430
well into what we're
putting together

00:29:16.430 --> 00:29:18.440
and how we're
approaching things.

00:29:18.440 --> 00:29:21.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

